      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, because the dose is unsafe as written.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6000 mg/day. That is 1.5 times the usual adult maximum of 4000 mg/day. Many institutions cap it at 3000 mg/day. A single 2000 mg dose is also twice the usual maximum of 1000 mg. This much acetaminophen risks serious hepatotoxicity, even in a patient with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some standard options for a 70 kg adult with normal liver and kidney function:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3000 mg/day)\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily** (4000 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2600 mg/day)\n\nDid you mean one of these, or is there a different dose or frequency you intended? Please also confirm the dose with your supervising resident or attending. Once you tell me which regimen you want, I'll enter it."
      }
